---
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";

/**
 * Section 06 on /security. Sources: docs/security-guarantees.md "Threat
 * Model", "Boundaries", "Recovery Key Disclosure", "Content Confidentiality",
 * "Held descendant re-citation"; docs/keying-design.md "Current access is
 * history-inclusive"; docs/developer/trusted-user-identity.md (pins are per
 * trust store, and resetting the local database resets them). The
 * #trust-boundaries id is linked from Home.
 */
const limits = [
  {
    term: "First contact establishes trust",
    body: "Each device pins the first identity bundle it accepts and rejects later changes. Adding someone to Contacts, the roster, or a group, or sharing a folder with them, requires their identity link, whose keys are checked before anything is added, so send links through a channel you already trust. Checking a signer still trusts the service's first answer, and a server that substitutes a key there can show a false signer, though that key cannot be used to add anyone. A link does not prove that a key is still current.",
  },
  {
    term: "Checkpoints cover what a device has seen",
    body: "A device with no checkpoint for a policy or folder (a new device, a folder or organization it is seeing for the first time, or a device whose local data was reset) can receive old but validly signed history. Signatures alone do not prove that a view is the newest or that all devices see the same view. Stronger split-view resistance needs independent witnessing or gossip.",
  },
  {
    term: "Availability depends on the service",
    body: "The service can withhold ciphertext, policies, or key envelopes and refuse requests. When required verification material is missing, the operation cannot safely proceed, so it stops.",
  },
  {
    term: "Unlocked devices are trusted",
    body: "Local encryption does not protect against malicious code running in an unlocked client or a compromised device. Anyone who obtains the recovery phrase, including its QR code, can recover that identity.",
  },
  {
    term: "Members see a folder's retained history",
    body: "Access includes history: a member added to a container can read the history that container retains, not only changes made after they joined.",
  },
];
---

<section class="site-section security-section" id="trust-boundaries" aria-labelledby="trust-title">
  <SectionHeader
    index="06"
    titleId="trust-title"
    title="Scope"
    lede="Where the guarantees above apply, and what sits outside them, under the same assumptions."
  />
  <dl class="site-spec security-limits">
    {limits.map((limit) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{limit.term}</dt>
        <dd>{limit.body}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="security-revocation">
    <h3>Revocation, nested containers, and historical access</h3>
    <div class="site-prose">
      <p>
        Revocation cannot retract keys someone already learned or content they
        already downloaded. Rotating a parent container does not automatically
        rekey every nested container, and refreshing a nested container's signed
        access history does not itself replace its encryption keys or promise
        confidentiality from a former member.
      </p>
      <p>
        A revoked signer working with a compromised server may still present
        records under valid historical authority to a device that has not seen
        newer signed state. Verifying that authority does not prove when the
        record was actually created.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current container keys also unlock retained predecessor keys, so current
        members can read history. A compromised current container key therefore
        exposes that retained history.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
